1
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:03,710
He really looks exactly like him.

2
00:00:03,710 --> 00:00:05,250
<i>But they're nothing alike.</i>

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:06,810
<i>I'm such a fool.</i>

4
00:00:06,810 --> 00:00:10,520
<i>Of course Master Seki wouldn't be here.</i>

5
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:13,160
I want to go home.

6
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:17,090
I want to go home.

7
00:00:25,820 --> 00:00:27,870
What are you doing?

8
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:32,840
Ms. Nashiki?

9
00:00:33,870 --> 00:00:37,220
What were you doing just now?

10
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:40,900
No, there's been a misunderstanding!

11
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,200
Please, listen to me!

12
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:43,930
Ms. Nashiki!

13
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:51,110
I'm so sorry!

14
00:00:51,110 --> 00:00:54,030
I didn't mean to!

15
00:00:58,530 --> 00:01:06,590
[Music Room (2)]

16
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:13,970
Ms. Nashiki!

17
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:16,800
You came to watch today?

18
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:18,650
You seemed busy recently.

19
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:21,930
We all saw you practicing
for the Sports Festival, Ms. Nashiki.

20
00:01:21,930 --> 00:01:25,160
You were running so weird!

21
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:28,160
I saw her asking the lunch ladies
how they made their curry.

22
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:30,370
- <i>What?</i>
- Me too! That was hilarious!

23
00:01:30,370 --> 00:01:32,740
Why did you ask?

24
00:01:32,740 --> 00:01:34,780
It's just curry!

25
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:40,580
Ms. Nashiki?

26
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:43,990
- May 13th...
- Are you okay?

27
00:01:45,770 --> 00:01:46,980
Is that today?

28
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:50,060
- Yeah.
- It is.

29
00:01:58,510 --> 00:02:01,180
- Ms. Nashiki?
- Are you okay?

30
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:03,420
What's wrong?

31
00:02:07,370 --> 00:02:09,000
Ms. Nashiki?

32
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,360
What's wrong?

33
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:12,730
You should go to the nurse.

34
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:15,120
No, I'm fine. I'm fine.

35
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:16,540
You all keep practicing.

36
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:18,150
Ms. Nashiki, you're acting strange.

37
00:02:18,150 --> 00:02:20,990
It's like you're back to the old Ms. Nashiki.

38
00:02:20,990 --> 00:02:24,710
You're not loud like you usually are.

39
00:02:24,710 --> 00:02:30,010
- Yeah.
- I liked the loud Ms. Nashiki.

40
00:02:33,870 --> 00:02:35,590
<i>I don't remember.</i>

41
00:02:35,590 --> 00:02:37,770
<i>I don't remember anything.</i>

42
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:39,420
<i>What have I been doing?</i>

43
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:41,720
<i>Where was I yesterday? The day before?</i>

44
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:45,030
Ms. Nashiki! Good afternoon.

45
00:02:45,030 --> 00:02:50,380
Will your class be using this map of Japan?

46
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:53,210
If not, I'll put it away.

47
00:02:53,210 --> 00:02:55,810
Mr. Majima.

48
00:02:56,360 --> 00:02:57,610
May I ask a question?

49
00:02:57,610 --> 00:03:00,070
Of course. What's up?

50
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:02,570
Have I been coming to school?

51
00:03:02,570 --> 00:03:04,870
Huh? What do you mean?

52
00:03:04,870 --> 00:03:09,780
This entire month, have I...

53
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:12,470
Of course you have.

54
00:03:17,210 --> 00:03:19,230
Is everything okay?
You seem fatigued.

55
00:03:19,230 --> 00:03:21,250
I'm sorry, it's just...

56
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:29,840
How have I been acting this month?

57
00:03:30,430 --> 00:03:32,460
How you've been acting? Well...

58
00:03:38,830 --> 00:03:43,100
You've certainly become more frank.

59
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:48,110
I can tell you're honest with your students.

60
00:03:48,110 --> 00:03:52,410
These days, it feels like...

61
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:56,150
You've really got yourself together.

62
00:03:59,490 --> 00:04:02,340
Your whole vibe has changed, too.

63
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:05,190
Did you recharge your batteries
over Golden Week?

64
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:08,860
<i>Who is he talking about?</i>

65
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:12,370
I knew it. How, a vacation?

66
00:04:12,370 --> 00:04:15,110
I'm sorry. I still have work to finish.

67
00:04:15,110 --> 00:04:18,050
Oh, right. See you around.

68
00:04:22,850 --> 00:04:25,420
<i>What happened to me?</i>

69
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:27,590
<i>Why can't I remember?</i>

70
00:04:53,670 --> 00:04:56,240
[There are two chickens in the garden]

71
00:05:03,980 --> 00:05:05,100
Something like that.

72
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:07,600
Saya, you're amazing!

73
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:09,680
You could become a ninja!

74
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:10,980
I'm not doing anything special.

75
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:13,510
- I'm serious.
- Okay, your turn.

76
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:20,450
I still don't get it.
Why do these give the same character?

77
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:23,280
Why are there two different inputs?

78
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:25,010
Is there an important difference?

79
00:05:25,010 --> 00:05:27,640
- No.
- What?

80
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:29,740
Just don't question it.

81
00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:31,680
- Don't think, feel.
- Huh?

82
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:34,220
That's the way it goes.

83
00:05:34,220 --> 00:05:36,000
I don't understand.

84
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,070
Third graders nowadays can all blind-type.

85
00:05:39,070 --> 00:05:40,600
So no complaining, and get typing.

86
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:41,780
- Yes, ma'am!
- Alright.

87
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:42,860
- Ready?
- Bring it.

88
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:44,080
Go.
[Start]

89
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:50,010
Your left hand. Left!

90
00:05:50,010 --> 00:05:52,140
Yeah, exactly!

91
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:53,180
You're so slow!

92
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:56,010
Ms. Nashiki, mind if we talk?
It's about school.

93
00:05:56,010 --> 00:05:58,030
Sure thing.

94
00:05:58,030 --> 00:06:00,110
Sorry, I'll be right back.

95
00:06:00,110 --> 00:06:02,400
I promise I'll do that later.

96
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:04,540
Just give us a minute.

97
00:06:09,170 --> 00:06:11,590
Am I managing to act normal?

98
00:06:12,260 --> 00:06:13,060
Yeah.

99
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:15,780
<i>Ms. Nashiki! Ms. Nashiki!</i>

100
00:06:17,180 --> 00:06:19,090
Excuse the question, but...

101
00:06:19,090 --> 00:06:21,890
Which one are you right now?

102
00:06:21,890 --> 00:06:23,310
Which one?

103
00:06:23,310 --> 00:06:24,690
What's one plus one?

104
00:06:24,690 --> 00:06:27,050
- Two.
- No, that doesn't prove anything.

105
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:30,810
Tell me something
only a person from Edo would know.

106
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:37,990
Us couriers were sometimes called
the Seventeenths.

107
00:06:40,490 --> 00:06:42,900
In any case, now we can be certain.

108
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:44,960
- Ms. Nashiki wasn't dead.
- Yeah.

109
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:47,690
Her consciousness was just asleep.

110
00:06:47,690 --> 00:06:53,550
In other words, if I leave this body,
Ms. Nashiki goes back to normal.

111
00:06:55,270 --> 00:06:57,900
Damn it all...

112
00:06:57,900 --> 00:07:01,660
I really do seem like an evil spirit.

113
00:07:01,660 --> 00:07:02,970
That's putting it lightly.

114
00:07:02,970 --> 00:07:08,020
Don't laugh. I bet everyone back home
is just as worried about me.

115
00:07:08,020 --> 00:07:10,150
I've got to get back soon.

116
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:13,250
Yeah.

117
00:07:18,530 --> 00:07:20,150
On my bed?

118
00:07:20,150 --> 00:07:23,150
How do I get home?

119
00:07:25,870 --> 00:07:28,100
Well...

120
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:35,740
The comet.

121
00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:37,510
When you first arrived, there was a comet.

122
00:07:37,510 --> 00:07:39,940
That one was only once every 341 years,

123
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:42,320
but maybe other astronomical phenomena
would do the trick.

124
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:43,580
Astronomical?

125
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:47,400
Meteor showers, eclipses, meteorites -
we have several possibilities.

126
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:50,070
Meteorites, meteorites...

127
00:07:50,070 --> 00:07:56,020
So you're saying if I'm on that rooftop
when one happens, I go home?

128
00:07:56,020 --> 00:07:57,610
Exactly!

129
00:07:58,540 --> 00:08:00,350
At least, I think so.

130
00:08:00,350 --> 00:08:02,620
The location is probably
another important factor.

131
00:08:02,620 --> 00:08:05,040
That building was nearby Konno Hachimangu.

132
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:08,920
Maybe you were there
during the Edo era as well.

133
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:23,510
[Astronomical phenomena - Location]
[Connection to Edo]

134
00:08:23,510 --> 00:08:26,100
Connected, how? Item...

135
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:29,180
[An item]

136
00:08:32,170 --> 00:08:36,210
Have you managed to solve
Master Seki's question?

137
00:08:37,860 --> 00:08:39,610
<i>Not yet.</i>

138
00:08:39,610 --> 00:08:41,960
I'm nowhere close to figuring it out.

139
00:08:45,610 --> 00:08:49,050
But I will solve his question.

140
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:52,050
- Still...
- Still...

141
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:55,080
You first.

142
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:58,870
Ms. Nashiki tried to die.

143
00:08:58,870 --> 00:09:03,490
Yes, and we have to figure out why.
Otherwise she might...

144
00:09:03,490 --> 00:09:04,950
We can't just "figure it out".

145
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:09,010
Ms. Nashiki has to want to live
of her own accord.

146
00:09:09,010 --> 00:09:12,220
I can't go back
without knowing she's alright.

147
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:20,290
First, let's work out what had driven
Ms. Nashiki to such extremes.

148
00:09:20,290 --> 00:09:21,730
Right.

149
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:24,980
Have you noticed anything
that would potentially bother her?

150
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:27,980
Unreasonable parents, kids who don't attend,
stuff like that.

151
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:30,850
A kid who doesn't attend...

152
00:09:30,850 --> 00:09:32,700
Actually, yeah. There is one kid
who never comes in.

153
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:35,230
Azuki Yoshida.

154
00:09:35,230 --> 00:09:37,330
Not here today either, huh?

155
00:09:37,330 --> 00:09:40,080
<i>How long haven't they been attending?</i>

156
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:43,350
At the very least, I haven't met them yet.

157
00:09:43,350 --> 00:09:46,020
That's a problem.

158
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:48,970
Is it?

159
00:09:48,970 --> 00:09:53,260
But farmers this time of year
need all the help they can get.

160
00:09:53,260 --> 00:09:55,820
- Kids sometimes stay back to help, and...
- No, no.

161
00:09:55,820 --> 00:09:57,480
That's not how it works now.

162
00:09:57,480 --> 00:10:00,400
School is mandatory for all kids.

163
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:01,890
But Saya doesn't go to school.

164
00:10:01,890 --> 00:10:03,340
Hey!

165
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:15,210
If Saya chooses not to attend,
then I respect her choice.

166
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:19,610
School isn't the only option
for kids her age.

167
00:10:20,490 --> 00:10:23,530
But your students are only third graders.

168
00:10:23,530 --> 00:10:27,160
It may not be their choice
that they're missing school.

169
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:31,160
Does that mean something
happened to them in April?

170
00:10:31,160 --> 00:10:35,890
Only the Ms. Nashiki
of the 21st century would know.

171
00:10:35,890 --> 00:10:38,560
I see.

172
00:10:39,240 --> 00:10:43,530
Thanks, Ikuru.
I didn't realize there was a real problem.

173
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:50,640
If only I shared memories with Ms. Nashiki.

174
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:53,310
I'd appreciate that, too.

175
00:10:53,310 --> 00:10:56,940
21st century Ms. Nashiki
clearly didn't trust me.

176
00:10:56,940 --> 00:10:59,050
She looked at me like I was vermin.

177
00:10:59,050 --> 00:11:00,660
Hey.

178
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:02,250
Is this...

179
00:11:03,570 --> 00:11:05,480
Oh, that? That's from when I was younger.

180
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:08,040
It's a picture of me and my mom.

181
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:11,440
But this...

182
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:13,050
The ocean?

183
00:11:14,690 --> 00:11:16,460
I knew it.

184
00:11:16,460 --> 00:11:19,090
So this is the ocean.

185
00:11:20,770 --> 00:11:24,640
I always heard about it from the couriers.

186
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:28,940
The water really does stretch forever!

187
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:33,430
Do you want to go?

188
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,750
Nowadays, it's pretty easy to get there.

189
00:11:38,750 --> 00:11:40,500
I'll take you sometime.

190
00:11:43,870 --> 00:11:45,720
Promise?

191
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:47,150
Promise.

192
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:56,230
<i>Will Ms. Nashiki really...</i>

193
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:02,420
[Perseids Meteor Shower, August 13th]

194
00:12:05,300 --> 00:12:08,230
If she's not able to go home...

195
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:18,180
She'd stay here forever?

196
00:12:23,760 --> 00:12:26,730
[World Grand Prix of Poker]

197
00:12:26,730 --> 00:12:31,900
<i>At long last, the WGOP has finally begun.</i>

198
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:36,610
<i>One of Japan's very own young stars
is testing her skills here today.</i>

199
00:12:36,610 --> 00:12:41,840
<i>What I really want isn't something
that can be bought with money.</i>

200
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:47,990
<i>So I'll make it my own,
using nothing but my own two hands.</i>

201
00:12:57,910 --> 00:12:59,450
Ms. Iroha.

202
00:12:59,990 --> 00:13:03,040
I don't know a thing about you.

203
00:13:04,290 --> 00:13:09,440
But I can tell
you were working hard every day.

204
00:13:11,270 --> 00:13:16,080
Because these notes,
they only mention the good.

205
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:19,620
<i>They make no note of the bad.</i>

206
00:13:19,620 --> 00:13:21,730
<i>Or of what hurt you.</i>

207
00:13:22,950 --> 00:13:26,170
You weren't writing down the truth.

208
00:13:27,450 --> 00:13:31,500
Were you trying to lie,
even to your own heart?

209
00:13:51,280 --> 00:13:54,690
[Dear Ms. Iroha Nashiki]

210
00:14:05,010 --> 00:14:07,230
Morning!

211
00:14:07,230 --> 00:14:09,530
Cute!

212
00:14:09,530 --> 00:14:11,600
I've never seen that sticker before!

213
00:14:11,600 --> 00:14:12,590
Let's trade!

214
00:14:12,590 --> 00:14:16,990
But this sticker is super rare.

215
00:14:17,650 --> 00:14:22,890
Okay, then I'll trade your Mametchi,
for this one!

216
00:14:22,890 --> 00:14:26,420
That one is so small.

217
00:14:27,310 --> 00:14:29,280
Fine, I'll trade two for that one.

218
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:30,500
Okay!

219
00:14:31,080 --> 00:14:32,440
Here.

220
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:38,570
Midori, do you also wanna trade?

221
00:14:38,570 --> 00:14:39,980
Yeah.

222
00:14:41,610 --> 00:14:43,830
Hey, your stickers aren't puffy.

223
00:14:43,830 --> 00:14:45,920
Normal stickers aren't worth much.

224
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:46,940
Yeah.

225
00:14:46,940 --> 00:14:50,170
But I feel bad not trading.

226
00:14:50,170 --> 00:14:55,300
Here, you can have this Miraitchi
if I can have that entire sheet.

227
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:57,840
- The whole thing?
- Yeah, you only have normal stickers.

228
00:14:57,840 --> 00:15:00,790
Puffy stickers are worth way more.

229
00:15:00,790 --> 00:15:03,780
You didn't know, Midori?

230
00:15:04,540 --> 00:15:05,990
Here.

231
00:15:08,570 --> 00:15:09,910
Lucky!

232
00:15:09,910 --> 00:15:11,880
You're so nice, Ichika.

233
00:15:11,880 --> 00:15:15,240
I'll always trade with a friend.

234
00:15:15,240 --> 00:15:16,980
Thanks.

235
00:15:21,430 --> 00:15:23,400
- Look at this one.
- Cute!

236
00:15:23,400 --> 00:15:24,950
It was the last one.

237
00:15:24,950 --> 00:15:26,270
Seriously?

238
00:15:26,270 --> 00:15:27,650
I waited three and a half hours for it.

239
00:15:27,650 --> 00:15:29,300
No way.

240
00:15:29,300 --> 00:15:33,580
Now then, only one month remains
until the Sports Festival.

241
00:15:33,580 --> 00:15:37,170
I hope preparations are going smoothly
for each grade.

242
00:15:37,170 --> 00:15:42,170
A representative from the Ministry
will come to observe, so please be aware.

243
00:15:42,170 --> 00:15:45,720
The Ministry is coming to observe?

244
00:15:45,720 --> 00:15:47,010
But why our school?

245
00:15:47,010 --> 00:15:51,190
That is because I am the principal here.

246
00:15:51,190 --> 00:15:54,260
Only 25% of schools in Japan
are led by women.

247
00:15:54,260 --> 00:15:56,920
If I am able to be a shining example,

248
00:15:56,920 --> 00:16:00,860
it will encourage other women
to aim for managerial positions.

249
00:16:04,290 --> 00:16:05,980
Inspiring words, ma'am.

250
00:16:05,980 --> 00:16:08,080
But the Principal isn't married, is she?

251
00:16:08,080 --> 00:16:10,540
Personally, I plan to marry, and have kids.

252
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:12,960
I'm counting on my kids
to care for me at her age.

253
00:16:12,960 --> 00:16:15,000
- Yikes.
- To really make us shine,

254
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,280
all faculty will be performing
the Soran Bushi sea shanty

255
00:16:19,280 --> 00:16:22,450
alongside our sixth graders.

256
00:16:22,450 --> 00:16:25,190
I'm counting on you all to get practicing!

257
00:16:26,270 --> 00:16:29,290
Come on, let's get pumped, folks!

258
00:16:29,290 --> 00:16:30,380
Repeat after me.

259
00:16:30,380 --> 00:16:32,940
Heave-ho! Heave-ho!

260
00:16:32,940 --> 00:16:34,920
Heave-ho! Heave-ho!

261
00:16:34,920 --> 00:16:36,610
Soran, soran!

262
00:16:36,610 --> 00:16:37,920
Soran, soran!

263
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:39,650
Excuse me.

264
00:16:39,650 --> 00:16:41,380
Yes, Kamiya?

265
00:16:41,380 --> 00:16:44,350
I believe mandatory participation
is a form of workplace harassment.

266
00:16:44,350 --> 00:16:47,440
No, no. This is no harassment.

267
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:49,130
This is for the Sports Festival!

268
00:16:49,130 --> 00:16:55,520
It is an activity for us faculty!

269
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:56,650
Right?

270
00:16:56,650 --> 00:16:59,440
- Right, Ms. Nashiki?
- Huh? Well...

271
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:03,780
- Yes, Soran.
- Ma'am, what's a "soran"?

272
00:17:03,780 --> 00:17:07,160
Heave-ho! Heave-ho!

273
00:17:07,160 --> 00:17:11,040
- Soran, soran!
- Oh, like a festival dance?

274
00:17:11,040 --> 00:17:15,240
- Seems like fun.
- Right, fun! Very fun!

275
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:20,590
We'll hold a kickoff party this Friday
for all faculty.

276
00:17:20,590 --> 00:17:22,210
Yes, ma'am!

277
00:17:22,210 --> 00:17:24,430
Soran, soran!

278
00:17:24,430 --> 00:17:27,830
Heave-ho! Heave-ho!

279
00:17:27,830 --> 00:17:29,910
Soran, soran!

280
00:17:29,910 --> 00:17:32,700
Why do we have to do the Soran, too?

281
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:34,600
Yeah...

282
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:37,530
We're already busy with the third grade
self-produced dance.

283
00:17:37,530 --> 00:17:40,050
Self-produced dance?

284
00:17:41,880 --> 00:17:44,680
Listen up, everyone.

285
00:17:44,680 --> 00:17:49,170
Since I'm not too good with these tablets,
I called Mr. Majima to help out.

286
00:17:49,170 --> 00:17:50,900
Mr. Majima, if you'd please.

287
00:17:50,900 --> 00:17:52,270
Right.

288
00:17:52,270 --> 00:17:54,410
First, I want everyone to find a dance

289
00:17:54,410 --> 00:17:58,260
that's trending on Instagram, or Youtube.

290
00:17:58,260 --> 00:18:02,010
I'll give you all 10 minutes to search.

291
00:18:02,010 --> 00:18:05,280
Ready? Begin.

292
00:18:05,280 --> 00:18:06,910
Thank you for helping.

293
00:18:06,910 --> 00:18:08,830
Don't thank me just yet.

294
00:18:08,830 --> 00:18:12,080
Letting them search freely
is the difficult part.

295
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:14,340
Mr. Majima, how should we search?

296
00:18:14,340 --> 00:18:15,480
Good question.

297
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:18,780
First, practice typing in some keywords

298
00:18:18,780 --> 00:18:21,740
for what you're looking for.

299
00:18:21,740 --> 00:18:24,390
For example, "Sports Festival Dance",

300
00:18:24,390 --> 00:18:27,340
or "Third Grader Tries to Dance".

301
00:18:27,340 --> 00:18:30,290
Try these words out, and see what you find.

302
00:18:30,290 --> 00:18:31,380
[TubeKing]

303
00:18:31,380 --> 00:18:35,710
When you find something interesting,
send the URL here.

304
00:19:00,730 --> 00:19:02,880
What're you looking at, Midori?

305
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:07,160
Those are some pretty stones.

306
00:19:07,160 --> 00:19:09,630
No looking up anything irrelevant.

307
00:19:09,630 --> 00:19:12,460
You all have eight minutes left.

308
00:19:18,290 --> 00:19:20,630
All students in attendance sent their URLs,

309
00:19:20,630 --> 00:19:23,220
so I'll sort them alongside my class.

310
00:19:23,220 --> 00:19:25,320
Thank you.

311
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:28,060
Mr. Majima.

312
00:19:28,060 --> 00:19:30,040
You were so deft.

313
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:32,290
- Almost like a ninja.
- Huh?

314
00:19:32,290 --> 00:19:34,390
A ninja?

315
00:19:34,390 --> 00:19:37,540
You're able to type on that tablet,

316
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:42,100
all while instructing the kids.

317
00:19:42,100 --> 00:19:47,270
Almost made me want to pay you.

318
00:19:47,270 --> 00:19:49,350
You really are strange, Ms. Nashiki.

319
00:19:49,350 --> 00:19:52,540
- Hey! No running in the halls!
- Yes, Ms. Nashiki.

320
00:19:52,540 --> 00:19:58,640
I've never met someone who didn't know
about YouTube or Instagram before.

321
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:00,540
Sorry, I'll study up on them.

322
00:20:00,540 --> 00:20:04,540
No, I'm not saying that's a bad thing.

323
00:20:04,540 --> 00:20:08,220
If anything, it's nice.

324
00:20:08,220 --> 00:20:09,270
Huh?

325
00:20:09,270 --> 00:20:11,580
Oh, never mind.

326
00:20:11,580 --> 00:20:13,600
Let's get to our classes.

327
00:20:14,480 --> 00:20:17,540
One more thing, Mr. Majima.

328
00:20:17,540 --> 00:20:21,540
I need to talk to you about something.

329
00:20:24,250 --> 00:20:27,010
I'll meet you on the roof after school.

330
00:20:27,010 --> 00:20:28,390
- Let's go!
- Yeah!

331
00:20:28,390 --> 00:20:32,050
Hey! I said no running!

332
00:20:35,630 --> 00:20:37,020
Okay!

333
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:42,670
Wrong way.

334
00:20:42,670 --> 00:20:46,310
Midori. You were looking up
stickers earlier, weren't you?

335
00:20:46,310 --> 00:20:47,760
No.

336
00:20:47,760 --> 00:20:49,140
Didn't you know?

337
00:20:49,140 --> 00:20:53,040
Stickers sold online are all fake.

338
00:20:53,040 --> 00:20:56,830
No one will trade for fakes.

339
00:20:56,830 --> 00:20:59,000
Get your mom to buy you some!

340
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,300
Ichika, let's go.

341
00:21:01,300 --> 00:21:02,720
Yeah.

342
00:21:02,720 --> 00:21:04,490
Come on.

343
00:21:04,490 --> 00:21:07,300
Let's trade more stickers later!

344
00:21:26,680 --> 00:21:28,030
Mr. Majima.

345
00:21:28,030 --> 00:21:29,930
Have all your students gone home?

346
00:21:31,050 --> 00:21:31,790
Yeah.

347
00:21:31,790 --> 00:21:34,740
The third and fourth grade teachers
have to settle details for the relay race.

348
00:21:34,740 --> 00:21:39,230
Sorry, I have something to attend to first.

349
00:22:14,760 --> 00:22:16,200
Mr. Majima.

350
00:22:16,200 --> 00:22:18,460
Thanks for coming, I know you're busy.

351
00:22:18,460 --> 00:22:20,100
No, it's fine.

352
00:22:20,680 --> 00:22:22,210
So...

353
00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:26,570
What is it you wished to talk about?

354
00:22:26,570 --> 00:22:28,090
Well...

355
00:22:29,210 --> 00:22:32,490
How to put it...

356
00:22:38,170 --> 00:22:44,710
It seems acting normal at school is advised.

357
00:22:45,380 --> 00:22:48,540
Only the Principal needs to be informed.

358
00:22:48,540 --> 00:22:53,690
Then one or the other will be transferred
to another school.

359
00:22:54,240 --> 00:22:56,120
What are you talking about?

360
00:22:57,020 --> 00:23:02,190
I was only giving an example
of what could happen.

361
00:23:02,190 --> 00:23:04,970
What I wanted to ask you...

362
00:23:06,080 --> 00:23:10,770
Is what I was like in April.

363
00:23:10,770 --> 00:23:13,520
Huh? What you were like in April?

364
00:23:13,520 --> 00:23:15,950
Yeah. Did I cause any trouble?

365
00:23:15,950 --> 00:23:18,770
Why are you asking me?
You would know best, right?

366
00:23:18,770 --> 00:23:22,300
Actually, I can't seem to remember.

367
00:23:22,300 --> 00:23:24,080
What?

368
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:25,950
You don't remember?

369
00:23:29,340 --> 00:23:31,310
No wonder you said that the other day.

370
00:23:31,310 --> 00:23:32,880
Said, what?

371
00:23:32,880 --> 00:23:36,970
You asked if you were coming to school.

372
00:23:42,970 --> 00:23:45,440
Sorry for asking something so weird.

373
00:23:53,860 --> 00:23:56,650
Where did you go on vacation
over Golden Week?

374
00:23:56,650 --> 00:23:59,320
Vacation?

375
00:23:59,320 --> 00:24:03,420
Wherever you went completely changed you.

376
00:24:04,640 --> 00:24:06,130
No, I...

377
00:24:07,030 --> 00:24:11,560
I like it here.

378
00:24:14,210 --> 00:24:17,640
I always wanted to become a teacher.

379
00:24:17,640 --> 00:24:21,360
It was something I thought
I could only dream of.

380
00:24:22,940 --> 00:24:25,840
So this is already the place of my dreams.

381
00:24:27,350 --> 00:24:31,440
I wanted to become a teacher, and now I am.

382
00:24:31,440 --> 00:24:34,490
I couldn't ask for anything more.

383
00:24:38,320 --> 00:24:40,020
I see.

384
00:24:40,910 --> 00:24:45,150
So you must have remembered
why you became a teacher in the first place.

385
00:24:47,570 --> 00:24:53,020
I actually did my student teaching
at the elementary school I attended.

386
00:24:53,020 --> 00:24:56,580
At the time,
I marveled at how small the tables were,

387
00:24:56,580 --> 00:24:58,310
how perfect teachers were, and...

388
00:24:58,310 --> 00:25:00,180
Mr. Majima.

389
00:25:00,180 --> 00:25:01,470
Yes?

390
00:25:01,470 --> 00:25:05,640
One of my students isn't attending school.

391
00:25:05,640 --> 00:25:07,380
Oh...

392
00:25:07,380 --> 00:25:09,620
The kid who stopped coming
at the end of April?

393
00:25:09,620 --> 00:25:11,580
Yes, them.

394
00:25:12,470 --> 00:25:16,960
Some kids tend to burn out in April.

395
00:25:16,960 --> 00:25:20,330
Just give them time. It'll be fine.

396
00:25:20,330 --> 00:25:22,050
I see.

397
00:25:24,840 --> 00:25:28,160
Gosh, I can't help it.

398
00:25:28,160 --> 00:25:30,920
I keep relying on you, don't I?

399
00:25:30,920 --> 00:25:33,000
Thank you, Mr. Majima.

400
00:25:36,300 --> 00:25:38,650
Ms. Nashiki.

401
00:25:38,650 --> 00:25:39,980
Yes?

402
00:25:43,080 --> 00:25:48,170
Would you like to
get dinner together sometime?

403
00:25:48,170 --> 00:25:49,980
Together?

404
00:25:56,880 --> 00:25:58,420
Sorry for interrupting.

405
00:25:58,420 --> 00:26:00,870
I wonder who left these buckets here.

406
00:26:00,870 --> 00:26:02,920
What are you doing here, Mr. Ichinose?

407
00:26:02,920 --> 00:26:05,900
Well, you know.

408
00:26:08,440 --> 00:26:10,740
I came to see the blue skies.

409
00:26:10,740 --> 00:26:12,530
It's cloudy.

410
00:26:12,530 --> 00:26:15,180
If it's dinner you're asking about,
there's the faculty party this Friday.

411
00:26:15,180 --> 00:26:18,850
Great timing, right? Great timing indeed.

412
00:26:18,850 --> 00:26:20,540
Mr. Ichinose.

413
00:26:20,540 --> 00:26:24,920
You and Ms. Nashiki
have been quite close recently.

414
00:26:24,920 --> 00:26:25,730
No.

415
00:26:25,730 --> 00:26:30,150
Pardon me for asking, but are you two...

416
00:26:31,310 --> 00:26:32,680
In a relationship?

417
00:26:32,680 --> 00:26:36,720
No, no! Of course not!

418
00:26:36,720 --> 00:26:38,020
Right, Ms. Nashiki?

419
00:26:38,020 --> 00:26:41,130
Yeah. I just stay with Ikuru.

420
00:26:41,130 --> 00:26:42,710
Huh? Stay with him?

421
00:26:42,710 --> 00:26:44,580
No, no!

422
00:26:44,580 --> 00:26:47,900
She meant to say, uh, smile with him!

423
00:26:47,900 --> 00:26:49,880
The hell do you mean, smile?

424
00:26:49,880 --> 00:26:53,350
Ms. Nashiki, we need you. Come with me!

425
00:26:53,350 --> 00:26:54,870
- What now?
- Excuse us!

426
00:26:54,870 --> 00:26:56,960
Hey!

427
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:02,050
"Ikuru", is it.

428
00:27:03,020 --> 00:27:04,610
That scared me.

429
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:07,130
Why'd you run away?

430
00:27:07,130 --> 00:27:09,720
I couldn't help it!

431
00:27:09,720 --> 00:27:11,160
Excuse us.

432
00:27:16,290 --> 00:27:19,670
Right. I asked around about Ms. Nashiki.

433
00:27:19,670 --> 00:27:25,090
Apparently, she barely spoke
to our other staff in April.

434
00:27:25,090 --> 00:27:27,980
Ms. Nashiki?

435
00:27:29,300 --> 00:27:33,180
Well, we tried making conversation with her.

436
00:27:33,180 --> 00:27:35,240
Yeah, she seemed to be on edge all the time.

437
00:27:35,240 --> 00:27:38,990
We'd advise her to relax, and loosen up.

438
00:27:38,990 --> 00:27:42,960
But we didn't want to make it seem like
we were cautioning her.

439
00:27:42,960 --> 00:27:45,240
- I didn't want to be accused of harassment.
- Right.

440
00:27:45,240 --> 00:27:49,660
But part of teaching is dealing with
the unreasonable.

441
00:27:49,660 --> 00:27:51,760
Well, that's another issue entirely.

442
00:27:51,760 --> 00:27:54,970
Ms. Nashiki had just returned
from a year long break from work.

443
00:27:54,970 --> 00:28:00,100
She may have been worried
about the impression she left others.

444
00:28:04,320 --> 00:28:06,870
That must have been rough.

445
00:28:06,870 --> 00:28:09,770
I wonder if she had anyone to confide in...

446
00:28:10,960 --> 00:28:12,630
What about you, Ikuru?

447
00:28:13,570 --> 00:28:14,530
Huh?

448
00:28:14,530 --> 00:28:17,430
Were you close with Ms. Nashiki?

449
00:28:19,510 --> 00:28:20,830
No.

450
00:28:28,850 --> 00:28:30,380
What's wrong?

451
00:28:31,860 --> 00:28:33,110
Nothing.

452
00:28:33,110 --> 00:28:34,770
- Let's get back to work.
- Yeah.

453
00:28:34,770 --> 00:28:36,130
You move.

454
00:28:36,130 --> 00:28:37,570
- I can't.
- Stop kicking me.

455
00:28:37,570 --> 00:28:40,470
No fighting, you two.

456
00:28:44,960 --> 00:28:47,980
[S-Ward School's third grade teacher
wasn't working last year. Yikes!]

457
00:28:54,490 --> 00:28:57,970
[Class 3 Moms' Group Chat]

458
00:29:01,510 --> 00:29:03,300
[Did you hear?]

459
00:29:03,300 --> 00:29:05,350
[Ms. Nashiki hasn't confiscated
sticker albums from her students]

460
00:29:05,350 --> 00:29:07,620
[Really?]
[But toys aren't allowed in school]

461
00:29:07,620 --> 00:29:10,710
[She needs to reassess her responsibilities]
Mom, I'm hungry.

462
00:29:11,330 --> 00:29:13,350
Mom?

463
00:29:13,350 --> 00:29:16,850
Does everyone have their drinks?

464
00:29:16,850 --> 00:29:18,530
- Yes.
- Yup.

465
00:29:18,530 --> 00:29:23,750
Here's to our upcoming Sports Festival!

466
00:29:23,750 --> 00:29:24,910
Cheers!

467
00:29:24,910 --> 00:29:28,230
Cheers!

468
00:29:29,840 --> 00:29:35,180
We're also celebrating our two new hires
so let loose, and no holding back!

469
00:29:35,180 --> 00:29:38,550
- Thank you.
- Thank you!

470
00:29:42,030 --> 00:29:44,100
Ms. President, allow me to pour you a glass.

471
00:29:44,100 --> 00:29:48,910
Ichinose, I can tell you're a rookie.

472
00:29:48,910 --> 00:29:50,130
How so?

473
00:29:50,130 --> 00:29:53,770
At these sorts of functions,
we call the Principal, "President".

474
00:29:53,770 --> 00:29:56,320
- President?
- The Head of Year is your "Manager".

475
00:29:56,320 --> 00:29:58,860
We never know when parents
are listening, after all.

476
00:29:58,860 --> 00:30:03,100
- I see.
- Walls always have ears.

477
00:30:03,100 --> 00:30:04,920
And parents are hidden in the masses.

478
00:30:04,920 --> 00:30:06,500
I appreciate the advice.

479
00:30:06,500 --> 00:30:09,610
That makes parents ninjas, too.

480
00:30:09,610 --> 00:30:13,210
Huh? You're both drinking tea?

481
00:30:13,210 --> 00:30:15,050
Yes. I won't be drinking alcohol today.

482
00:30:15,050 --> 00:30:19,000
Oh, come on. If you can drink, drink!

483
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:20,520
As I said, let loose.

484
00:30:20,520 --> 00:30:23,980
Treat this party like an icebreaker!

485
00:30:24,500 --> 00:30:26,970
No, Ms. Akutsu...

486
00:30:37,850 --> 00:30:39,520
Thank you.

487
00:30:45,700 --> 00:30:48,470
The hell is this?

488
00:30:48,470 --> 00:30:52,810
It's yellow, but it's a fine brew!

489
00:30:52,810 --> 00:30:54,500
That's a western alcoholic drink.

490
00:30:54,500 --> 00:30:56,920
Western alcohol?

491
00:30:56,920 --> 00:30:59,480
Westerners know their stuff!

492
00:30:59,480 --> 00:31:03,060
Has she never had beer before?

493
00:31:03,060 --> 00:31:05,560
Or are you more a fan of Japanese sake?

494
00:31:06,120 --> 00:31:08,240
I think there was some on the menu...

495
00:31:08,240 --> 00:31:10,950
Nah, I like this stuff.

496
00:31:10,950 --> 00:31:12,530
May I have another glass?

497
00:31:14,390 --> 00:31:16,010
Thank you.

498
00:31:19,880 --> 00:31:21,310
I feel my throat pricking!

499
00:31:21,310 --> 00:31:22,320
We call that "sparkling".

500
00:31:22,320 --> 00:31:23,580
And this bitterness is divine!

501
00:31:23,580 --> 00:31:24,560
That's the hops.

502
00:31:24,560 --> 00:31:25,890
And these bubbles...

503
00:31:25,890 --> 00:31:28,190
You've got some there!
Avoid that, it's seen as cunning.

504
00:31:28,190 --> 00:31:30,240
- Huh?
- There, above your lip.

505
00:31:30,240 --> 00:31:33,160
Now hold on just a second.

506
00:31:33,160 --> 00:31:36,230
- Yes?
- You two seem close.

507
00:31:36,230 --> 00:31:37,570
Huh?

508
00:31:37,570 --> 00:31:39,110
No, no.

509
00:31:39,110 --> 00:31:45,390
Oh? Already? Am I going to have to do it?

510
00:31:45,390 --> 00:31:48,600
Am I sending one of you away next year?

511
00:31:48,600 --> 00:31:49,910
- Sorry!
- Hey!

512
00:31:49,910 --> 00:31:52,320
So sorry! The plates are safe.

513
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:54,740
Who cares about the damn plates.

514
00:31:55,650 --> 00:31:58,890
You almost got jijimi sauce on Rinma.

515
00:31:58,890 --> 00:32:00,360
Rinma?

516
00:32:01,650 --> 00:32:06,110
Oh, that jerk from "Mr. Butler"?
Think I've seen him around...

517
00:32:11,810 --> 00:32:14,050
- Say that again.
- Huh?

518
00:32:14,980 --> 00:32:17,060
Surely I was just hearing things, right?

519
00:32:17,060 --> 00:32:20,430
Surely you didn't disrespect his name.

520
00:32:20,430 --> 00:32:22,490
Huh? No, I...

521
00:32:23,660 --> 00:32:27,000
Rinma Endo has a name.

522
00:32:27,810 --> 00:32:30,080
So use it.

523
00:32:30,080 --> 00:32:35,170
Mr. Rinma Endo.

524
00:32:35,690 --> 00:32:37,180
Thank you.

525
00:32:42,420 --> 00:32:46,490
It was far too early to turn you to ashes...

526
00:32:46,490 --> 00:32:48,000
Help, she's scaring me!

527
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:52,050
Gosh, I'm glad our teachers
are looking out for each other.

528
00:32:52,050 --> 00:32:53,320
- How?
- And you're wrong!

529
00:32:53,320 --> 00:32:55,740
Then let me ask the group.

530
00:32:55,740 --> 00:33:02,400
Who here... has kissed in a classroom!

531
00:33:02,400 --> 00:33:04,370
Ms. Principal!

532
00:33:04,370 --> 00:33:07,320
We're breaking ice!

533
00:33:07,320 --> 00:33:10,270
Come on, be honest!

534
00:33:10,270 --> 00:33:13,980
Anyone? Anyone?

535
00:33:16,700 --> 00:33:20,910
Could we get another order
of chilled tomatoes?

536
00:33:20,910 --> 00:33:22,980
Chilled tomatoes, coming right up!

537
00:33:23,900 --> 00:33:26,130
- Would you all like some, too?
- Yes, please!

538
00:33:26,130 --> 00:33:28,570
- Please make that three chilled tomatoes!
- Sure thing!

539
00:33:36,610 --> 00:33:39,600
Here are your chilled tomatoes.

540
00:33:39,600 --> 00:33:41,700
Hey!

541
00:33:41,700 --> 00:33:44,250
Eat slower! This isn't our lunch break!

542
00:33:44,250 --> 00:33:46,480
Say what?

543
00:33:46,480 --> 00:33:49,880
Eating fast is ingrained in us teachers.

544
00:33:49,880 --> 00:33:52,990
Really, we should be getting paid
for attending this party.

545
00:33:52,990 --> 00:33:54,810
Two more samgyeopsal's, please.

546
00:33:54,810 --> 00:33:56,740
If you're only going to complain, don't come.

547
00:33:56,740 --> 00:33:58,360
I'd be told off if I didn't.

548
00:33:58,360 --> 00:33:59,530
That's also harassment.

549
00:33:59,530 --> 00:34:04,040
Calling everything harassment
is also a form of harassment.

550
00:34:04,040 --> 00:34:06,350
- Exactly.
- Mr. Konishi.

551
00:34:06,350 --> 00:34:09,020
How'd it go with the dating app girl?

552
00:34:09,020 --> 00:34:13,870
Hear this. I had to stay after school,
and was late to one date,

553
00:34:13,870 --> 00:34:16,020
and she called our whole relationship off!

554
00:34:16,020 --> 00:34:17,090
What?

555
00:34:17,090 --> 00:34:20,210
Your sake.

556
00:34:20,210 --> 00:34:21,450
Sake!

557
00:34:21,450 --> 00:34:24,790
Stop, you've already reached your limit.

558
00:34:24,790 --> 00:34:26,480
You've drunk far too much.

559
00:34:26,480 --> 00:34:28,500
Hey!

560
00:34:28,500 --> 00:34:31,970
I know that thing! On your tag, there.

561
00:34:31,970 --> 00:34:34,940
I saw it earlier today. Such a doll!

562
00:34:34,940 --> 00:34:37,200
- A doll...
- Sorry about her.

563
00:34:37,200 --> 00:34:38,910
She means it's cute.

564
00:34:38,910 --> 00:34:41,420
Stickers, huh?

565
00:34:41,420 --> 00:34:43,600
I confiscated another binder today.

566
00:34:43,600 --> 00:34:48,100
Same. I have to remind my students
not to bring them every day.

567
00:34:48,100 --> 00:34:50,440
They're not allowed to bring them?

568
00:34:50,440 --> 00:34:51,610
Why not?

569
00:34:51,610 --> 00:34:53,280
Isn't that obvious?

570
00:34:53,280 --> 00:34:56,760
If we allowed it, there'd be a divide
between kids with stickers and without.

571
00:34:56,760 --> 00:35:00,480
Yeah. They'd start fighting,
saying it's unfair and all.

572
00:35:00,480 --> 00:35:02,260
Why?

573
00:35:02,260 --> 00:35:06,980
Let's say there's a kid
with tons of stickers.

574
00:35:06,980 --> 00:35:10,380
Seeing this,
another kid is sad they have none.

575
00:35:10,380 --> 00:35:12,480
If they're not all equal, that's just unfair.

576
00:35:12,480 --> 00:35:14,220
Unfair?

577
00:35:14,940 --> 00:35:19,470
Elementary school is a place where we aim
for all kids to be equal.

578
00:35:19,470 --> 00:35:22,790
It's also so our kids
maintain their self-esteem.

579
00:35:22,790 --> 00:35:25,550
Encouraging inequality does no one any good.

580
00:35:25,550 --> 00:35:27,090
Exactly.

581
00:35:29,360 --> 00:35:35,670
But what does equality really mean?

582
00:35:35,670 --> 00:35:36,890
Huh?

583
00:35:36,890 --> 00:35:40,730
I mean, this world is plenty unequal, right?

584
00:35:40,730 --> 00:35:42,430
Ms. Nashiki, that's no way to...

585
00:35:42,430 --> 00:35:44,250
- Ms. Principal.
- President.

586
00:35:44,250 --> 00:35:47,560
You agree with me, right?

587
00:35:47,560 --> 00:35:50,190
This world ain't equal.

588
00:35:50,190 --> 00:35:54,260
The child of the shogun
and the child of a peasant are different.

589
00:35:54,260 --> 00:35:56,620
Peasant?

590
00:35:56,620 --> 00:35:57,820
I see...

591
00:35:57,820 --> 00:35:59,640
Maybe ages ago, but...

592
00:35:59,640 --> 00:36:03,280
The child of a merchant
wears a quality kimono.

593
00:36:03,280 --> 00:36:07,890
While farming children are barefoot
even in the winter. That's a given.

594
00:36:07,890 --> 00:36:10,420
It's how things are.

595
00:36:10,420 --> 00:36:12,940
- Ms. Nashiki, please stop.
- I'm not wrong, am I?

596
00:36:12,940 --> 00:36:14,070
Right, sorry.

597
00:36:14,070 --> 00:36:16,500
And the child of a courier

598
00:36:16,500 --> 00:36:20,550
has their own life to live.

599
00:36:21,960 --> 00:36:24,250
No amount of complaining will change that.

600
00:36:24,250 --> 00:36:28,210
They feel it's unfair
to be compared to other kids?

601
00:36:28,210 --> 00:36:33,380
Then they oughta live the life
befitting their standing.

602
00:36:34,800 --> 00:36:37,630
I understand what you're trying to say.

603
00:36:37,630 --> 00:36:40,620
And honestly, you're right.

604
00:36:40,620 --> 00:36:47,140
The household you're born in
dictates the start you'll get in life.

605
00:36:47,140 --> 00:36:48,750
That's the reality of it all.

606
00:36:48,750 --> 00:36:51,790
Ms. Principal - I mean, President...

607
00:36:51,790 --> 00:36:56,270
But when we teachers strive for equality,

608
00:36:56,270 --> 00:37:01,040
we're not necessarily trying to change
the way the world works.

609
00:37:01,040 --> 00:37:03,940
The way the world works?

610
00:37:03,940 --> 00:37:09,840
School is a place where kids
spend a majority of their days at.

611
00:37:09,840 --> 00:37:13,450
It's a place to nurture their futures.

612
00:37:13,450 --> 00:37:21,630
Do you think it's our place to arbitrarily
decide who they're meant to be?

613
00:37:23,500 --> 00:37:26,390
Add these two horizontal bars,

614
00:37:26,390 --> 00:37:30,950
and you get a total of 399.

615
00:37:32,420 --> 00:37:33,660
Run.

616
00:37:33,660 --> 00:37:35,790
You three.

617
00:37:36,480 --> 00:37:38,080
Yes, you three there.

618
00:37:38,890 --> 00:37:40,780
Come here.

619
00:37:40,780 --> 00:37:42,440
- Should we?
- Come.

620
00:37:42,440 --> 00:37:44,460
Come here.

621
00:37:44,460 --> 00:37:45,940
Don't be shy.

622
00:37:46,380 --> 00:37:53,560
<i>♪ Align your calculating sticks,
and the path shall open ♪</i>

623
00:37:53,560 --> 00:37:56,940
<i>♪ It shall open wide for you ♪</i>

624
00:37:56,940 --> 00:38:00,890
<i>♪ Line your horizontals and verticals ♪</i>

625
00:38:00,890 --> 00:38:04,450
<i>♪ and you get your number ♪</i>

626
00:38:04,450 --> 00:38:08,760
<i>♪ The correct answer ♪</i>

627
00:38:12,310 --> 00:38:17,120
To bring it back to stickers,
kids with stickers aren't to blame.

628
00:38:17,120 --> 00:38:19,700
Neither are those without stickers.

629
00:38:19,700 --> 00:38:25,560
But when they're brought into the classroom
for all to see...

630
00:38:26,280 --> 00:38:30,560
The kids end up ranking themselves.

631
00:38:30,560 --> 00:38:34,600
Exactly. Each child mentally
starts ranking the others.

632
00:38:34,600 --> 00:38:38,150
And we want to prevent that from happening.

633
00:38:39,510 --> 00:38:44,080
<i>I do think it's important to live a life
appropriate of one's standing.</i>

634
00:38:44,080 --> 00:38:50,630
<i>But children know nothing
of their own "standing" yet.</i>

635
00:38:50,630 --> 00:38:57,970
And school can't be the place
where that arbitrary decision is made.

636
00:38:58,660 --> 00:39:02,690
It has to remain a place of equality.

637
00:39:04,310 --> 00:39:06,140
I see.

638
00:39:07,020 --> 00:39:09,120
I understand.

639
00:39:11,740 --> 00:39:15,770
Folks, I appreciate your guidance.

640
00:39:20,200 --> 00:39:24,290
That was a moving speech.

641
00:39:24,290 --> 00:39:26,480
Equal, as all schools should be!

642
00:39:26,480 --> 00:39:29,290
That's right. What a riveting ice breaker.

643
00:39:29,290 --> 00:39:34,600
Yes! I feel like my speech
was very inspiring.

644
00:39:34,600 --> 00:39:38,000
Did anyone record that?

645
00:39:38,000 --> 00:39:40,110
Me. I'm always recording.

646
00:39:40,110 --> 00:39:44,560
I won't dwell on the implications.

647
00:39:44,560 --> 00:39:48,280
In any case, rather than apologize,

648
00:39:48,280 --> 00:39:51,390
I'd appreciate if you put that effort
towards the Soran Bushi.

649
00:39:51,390 --> 00:39:53,900
The Soran Bushi...

650
00:39:53,900 --> 00:39:56,890
Right, of course! How could I forget?

651
00:39:56,890 --> 00:39:58,550
The entertainment!

652
00:39:58,550 --> 00:39:59,560
Entertainment?

653
00:39:59,560 --> 00:40:03,460
I did plenty of that for the Tokubei gang.

654
00:40:03,460 --> 00:40:05,880
Parties need their entertainment!

655
00:40:05,880 --> 00:40:08,940
G-Gang?

656
00:40:09,910 --> 00:40:13,130
I, Iroha of the Tokubei's,
shall now demonstrate!

657
00:40:15,010 --> 00:40:16,380
You can't!

658
00:40:16,380 --> 00:40:19,780
We must dance!

659
00:40:19,780 --> 00:40:21,540
Go, Ms. Nashiki!

660
00:40:21,540 --> 00:40:24,830
Heave-ho! Heave-ho!

661
00:40:24,830 --> 00:40:27,580
Don't look! No!

662
00:40:27,580 --> 00:40:30,750
- Ms. Nashiki! Stop that!
- Soran, soran!

663
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:40,450
Where is he? Found him!

664
00:40:48,940 --> 00:40:50,690
Ms. Akutsu?

665
00:40:50,690 --> 00:40:51,820
Mind if I record?

666
00:40:51,820 --> 00:40:54,260
Please don't! Don't!

667
00:40:54,260 --> 00:40:57,150
Ms. Nashiki, over here!

668
00:41:06,470 --> 00:41:08,030
Ms. Akutsu.

669
00:41:10,020 --> 00:41:12,220
Is Ms. Nashiki...

670
00:41:12,980 --> 00:41:15,910
the daughter of some gang?

671
00:41:15,910 --> 00:41:17,140
Huh?

672
00:41:17,140 --> 00:41:21,080
She claimed she was associated with one!

673
00:41:21,080 --> 00:41:22,820
Should we not get involved with her?

674
00:41:22,820 --> 00:41:24,580
Don't know.

675
00:41:24,580 --> 00:41:28,100
But if she is, I'm rooting for her
and the young boss.

676
00:41:28,100 --> 00:41:30,330
Young boss? Who?

677
00:41:31,020 --> 00:41:32,620
You're not making any sense.

678
00:41:33,890 --> 00:41:35,540
Wait, you mean...

679
00:41:38,330 --> 00:41:40,090
Is that what you mean?

680
00:41:41,040 --> 00:41:43,090
Is it?

681
00:41:46,900 --> 00:41:49,750
Looks like fireworks out there.

682
00:41:49,750 --> 00:41:52,150
Everything's sparkling.

683
00:41:52,150 --> 00:41:54,520
I'm not surprised, given how much you drank.

684
00:41:56,550 --> 00:41:59,310
Here, have some water.

685
00:42:07,380 --> 00:42:10,980
Ms. Nashiki, you can press that button.

686
00:42:18,690 --> 00:42:20,310
I can't believe you.

687
00:42:20,310 --> 00:42:22,790
No one belly dances like that nowadays.

688
00:42:22,790 --> 00:42:24,370
It's unheard of.

689
00:42:24,370 --> 00:42:27,180
Who cares? Not like it's doing me harm.

690
00:42:27,180 --> 00:42:29,150
It is though!

691
00:42:29,770 --> 00:42:31,180
Your skin...

692
00:42:33,810 --> 00:42:36,730
It's not something to show others.

693
00:42:36,730 --> 00:42:39,960
"Others"?

694
00:42:39,960 --> 00:42:43,090
Our coworkers are like family.

695
00:42:43,090 --> 00:42:44,640
No, they are not.

696
00:42:44,640 --> 00:42:46,620
What are you talking about?

697
00:42:47,950 --> 00:42:50,240
You need to be more cautious, Ms. Nashiki.

698
00:42:50,240 --> 00:42:53,150
That's why people misunderstand.

699
00:42:53,150 --> 00:42:55,410
Misunderstand?

700
00:42:55,410 --> 00:42:59,290
Would you like to
get dinner together sometime?

701
00:42:59,290 --> 00:43:02,000
Dinner?

702
00:43:03,050 --> 00:43:07,240
You can't be nice
to every last person you meet.

703
00:43:07,240 --> 00:43:09,120
Please learn some self-restraint.

704
00:43:09,120 --> 00:43:13,900
The hell? You act like I'm a lecher.

705
00:43:13,900 --> 00:43:15,980
A lecher?

706
00:43:15,980 --> 00:43:17,580
No, I just...

707
00:43:17,580 --> 00:43:20,330
I want you to stop acting so carelessly.

708
00:43:20,330 --> 00:43:22,290
Why?

709
00:43:22,290 --> 00:43:23,950
"Why"?

710
00:43:29,180 --> 00:43:33,720
Well... that's because...

711
00:43:44,450 --> 00:43:46,970
This is our stop.

712
00:43:48,380 --> 00:43:50,490
- Grab your things.
- Okay.

713
00:43:53,990 --> 00:43:55,610
Ikuru.

714
00:43:57,230 --> 00:43:59,000
Rinka?

715
00:43:59,580 --> 00:44:01,190
You came back?

716
00:44:01,190 --> 00:44:03,160
You didn't check your phone?

717
00:44:03,160 --> 00:44:04,610
My phone?

718
00:44:08,770 --> 00:44:11,480
Who's this?

719
00:44:11,480 --> 00:44:14,530
Oh, uh... this is my coworker.

720
00:44:14,530 --> 00:44:16,430
You're heading home with your coworker?

721
00:44:16,430 --> 00:44:18,530
That's 'cause we're friends.

722
00:44:18,530 --> 00:44:20,030
Right. We're friends.

723
00:44:20,030 --> 00:44:23,230
She's my neighbor.

724
00:44:23,230 --> 00:44:24,720
I see.

725
00:44:24,720 --> 00:44:30,480
If you're Ikuru's friend,
I'd like to be friends, too.

726
00:44:30,480 --> 00:44:32,850
- What's your name?
- Nashiki.

727
00:44:32,850 --> 00:44:35,390
Iroha Nashiki.

728
00:44:35,390 --> 00:44:37,170
- Iroha.
- Yes.

729
00:44:37,170 --> 00:44:38,960
Nice to meet you.

730
00:44:54,790 --> 00:44:59,080
I'm actually a bit tired today.

731
00:44:59,830 --> 00:45:03,220
Iroha. Let's talk again sometime.

732
00:45:03,220 --> 00:45:04,270
Yeah.

733
00:45:04,270 --> 00:45:06,860
You must be tired from the tournament.

734
00:45:06,860 --> 00:45:09,140
- Take care.
- Yeah.

735
00:45:09,140 --> 00:45:11,290
Ms. Nashiki, not that way.

736
00:45:11,290 --> 00:45:13,070
This way.

737
00:45:13,070 --> 00:45:17,400
Oh, right. Knowing you,
I bet you won. Congrats.

738
00:45:18,010 --> 00:45:19,490
See you around.

739
00:45:19,490 --> 00:45:21,080
You don't need to copy me.

740
00:45:21,080 --> 00:45:22,740
Watch your step.

741
00:45:22,740 --> 00:45:24,270
Come on.

742
00:45:25,530 --> 00:45:28,060
We're not crossing. This way.
